00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.010
So let's start with documents.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.450
So some of the most
important data in companies

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.640
isn't actually living
in databases but rather

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:49.270
in documents.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:50.812
So think about every
time that you've

00:02:50.812 --> 00:02:52.600
had to file an expense report.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.770
Nearly all business processes
either began, include,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.460
or end with a document.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.120
So this is where
Document AI comes in.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.880
This is a platform that
we launched at GA earlier

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.610
this year in April,
and it helps you

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.090
turn your unstructured
content into structured data.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.810
There's a lot of important
business information in PDFs

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:18.320
and images, and these
formats aren't actually

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.320
easily consumable to extract
data by a computer as it is.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.260
So this is what we
actually call dark data.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.260
So data that
companies have stored

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.900
for either compliance
reasons or something else,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.240
but you aren't
actually harnessing yet

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.790
to help decision making,
help customers or make

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.350
better business decisions.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.660
And this is what Document AI
is there to help you with.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.690
So there are lots
of different ways

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.400
that you can use Document AI.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.950
First we have
generic Document AI.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.250
So we actually have
a toolkit of OCR.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.800
We have OCR training
for type forms

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.780
as well as handwritten forms.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.700
This OCR is powerful.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.630
And then we even have something
called the form parser, which

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.500
will understand any form that
you feed it, it will understand

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.290
the question and answer
nature of that form

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.070
and feed it back to you, key
value pairs in a JSON format

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:16.100
that are much more
easy to use than doing

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.380
that parsing yourself from OCR.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.860
Then, in the middle, we have
specialized Document AI.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.230
So we have trained
several models

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:28.040
on the world's most common
business form types you

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.810
can leverage into your system.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.210
This is going to be the
focus of our talk today,

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.400
but let me also talk about
custom document processing.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.640
So we also have
tools in our toolkit

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.720
that you could train a model on
your proprietary business form

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.300
type so you can ensure accuracy.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.060
So specialized Document AI.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.820
We have different
solutions optimized

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.710
for different
industry use cases.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.210
Let me talk about that.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.370
First, we have
lending Document AI.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:05:00.550
So this is a set of specialized
models for forms such as W-2s,

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.395
W-9s, bank statements.

00:05:02.395 --> 00:05:03.770
All of these are
different pieces

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.450
that you'd have in a
typical mortgage packet.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:10.280
So using this solution, you can
streamline data capture and all

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.810
of the different steps that need
to happen in a mortgage process

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.610
to make it faster.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:19.850
Then, in the middle, we have
procurement Document AI.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:23.660
So this covers invoices,
receipts, utility statements

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:24.350
and more.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.460
And this is what we'll
be demoing today.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.310
But before we get
there, let me talk

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.680
about what we just
launched at Next this year,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.630
contracts Document AI.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.340
It's been said
that contracts can

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.460
be some of the most
important business documents

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.360
that you have.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.180
So we've trained
models to extract

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.520
key pieces of information
that you can then

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.830
use to make important
decisions, like

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:48.770
programmatically understanding
how long the contract is valid,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.490
things like that.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.820
So what's the magic in Doc AI?

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:54.560
It's not just OCR.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.100
It's a whole pipeline
leveraging the technology

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.130
that we've been building
at Google for decades.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:03.590
So first we have Doc Prep.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.800
So let's say you upload
an image of a wrinkled

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.720
or a coffee-stained document.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.010
We'll use image tools
to fix SKU, better

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:17.570
enhance the text so data can
be more easily extracted.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.120
Then we get to
split and classify.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.580
We want you to be
able to leverage

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.260
your data and the raw
form that it comes in.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.650
So think about a
large PDF packet

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:29.840
with several different
form types in it.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.810
Well, by using our splitting
and classifying tools,

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:36.350
you can actually identify
where these sub documents start

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.570
and end and what documents
are there so then

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:41.840
you can take those specific
pieces of your packet

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.150
and then feed them through
the correct processor.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.460
Then we have entity extraction.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.850
So this is where we extract
those key pieces of information

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.450
that you need per doc type.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.380
So even though you might
have high variance doc types,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.000
every receipt and invoice
might look different.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.010
We know that there are pieces of
information that are universal

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.550
to these document types.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.880
So for procurement,
every invoice

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.250
is going to have an
invoice ID, every receipt

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.700
will have several line items.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.310
They'll have currency.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.510
They'll have total amounts.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.050
They'll have tax values.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:17.370
So we've designed a
schema per form type

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.620
that you can guarantee
your data will

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.980
be merged into and returned
to you in your JSON response.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:25.740
So this makes it
much easier when

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.740
it's schematized to then
either feed into a database

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.110
or give the pieces
of information

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.318
that you need to a
downstream service.

00:07:34.318 --> 00:07:38.420
Now, let's talk about
some more magic pieces.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:42.150
Now we have
normalize and enrich.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.610
Have you ever been coding
with dates and times

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.680
and have a formatting mismatch?

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.810
I'm in America and
Guillaume's in France,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.880
and we use different
date-time formats.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.270
Well, Document AI
will do normalization

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.030
on monetary, dates,
and addresses

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.840
so it's more easy to
work with whatever style

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.800
your system is in.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.500
Here, we also use for enrichment
the Google knowledge graph.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.090
So the Google knowledge graph
is a service that powers search

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.010
as well as several
other applications

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.680
that lets you know contextual
relevant information.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.000
So let's say on your document
you have an incomplete business

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.320
name or maybe an address
that isn't full or correct.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.740
The Google knowledge graph will
supplement this information

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.930
with the key pieces
of information

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:32.570
that you could then
find in a search query.

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.429
Then, last but
not least, we know

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:36.799
with these critical
business processes,

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.450
you have no room for error.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.480
So with the
human-in-the-loop platform,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.650
you can select a confidence
threshold where you can have

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.380
a document reviewed if certain
keys don't meet a certain

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:52.400
value, say 95%, 98% confidence,
or if the whole document

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.080
doesn't meet that
confidence threshold.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.290
So I know we went through that
really fast, but now let's

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.750
work on taking this
API and turning it

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.582
into a full fledged application.

00:09:04.582 --> 00:09:06.290
So I'm going to hand
it over to Guillaume

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:09.073
to tell us how we're
going to do that.

00:09:09.073 --> 00:09:10.490
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Thank you, Anu.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.200
Anu described the big
piece of the puzzle, which

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.530
is where most of the smarts are,
but we'll need some business

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.710
logic, some business processes
to integrate everything

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:23.070
together, to create a full blown
useful application for our end

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:23.940
users.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.800
In a short moment
I'll show you how

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.470
we use Document AI, Cloud
Functions, and workflows

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.530
to craft a small
expense report solution.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.170
But how are we going to do it?

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:38.330
Well, we are going to use Google
Cloud's serverless solutions

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.840
for business logic thanks
to Cloud Functions.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.450
What is serverless,
and why should we

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.820
use that instead
of your homegrown

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.980
servers or virtual machines?

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:52.460
Serverless solutions allow you
to focus on your application

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:55.400
and not on infrastructure
management or provisioning.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.890
Serverless products
like Cloud Functions

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.540
fully manage the environments.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.870
Developers can use to
easily deploy and scale

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.880
their logic without hassle.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.010
So from a programming
model perspective,

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:09.020
we usually create
stateless services

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.650
based architectures,
which scale more simply.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.690
And last but not least,
since the scaling

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.010
is handled by the
platform for you,

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.420
we also apply only for
usage pricing model,

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:22.700
where you pay,
really, proportional

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:26.090
to the actual usage
of your application.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:29.420
Cloud Functions is a
bit like some cloud glue

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.610
for a more programmable cloud.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:34.100
Tying various useful
services together,

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.220
reacting through events
flying through your system.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:40.580
There are [INAUDIBLE] available
for all major programming

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.900
languages, whether it's
Node.js, Python, Java and more.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:49.370
Cloud Functions react to events,
like incoming HTTP request,

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:50.870
background events
from the cloud,

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:55.520
like a new file stored on
Cloud Storage or a new document

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.970
in Cloud Firestore,
our NoSQL database.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.690
And you can easily deploy
small units of business logic

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:05.540
on the platform from the
console, the Google Cloud

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:08.240
Console, or from the
command line as well.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:15.680
Applications are often composed
of several services or APIs

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.950
that you can call in turn to
create the overall business

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.730
process, like invoking
the Document AI API

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:27.770
or calling Firestore to store
some structured data as well

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.090
as some Cloud Functions
to implement some logic,

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.050
like finding the right
information from the Document

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.610
AI's output.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.670
For orchestrating those
APIs and services,

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:43.670
we can take advantage of
Google Cloud Workflows.

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:45.680
Workflows is a
solution to orchestrate

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.650
Google Cloud and
HTTP-based API services,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:50.900
including third party.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:56.360
It doesn't have to be something
solely hosted on Google Cloud,

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.150
and to tie them into
serverless workflows.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.970
It's about automating
complex processes.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.160
Workflow is a fully
managed service

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.300
requiring no infrastructure
or capacity planning.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.120
It offers fast
scalability support,

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.340
scaling down to
zero when not used

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:16.640
and a pay-per-use
pricing model per step

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.060
in the Workflow--
per step model.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.440
And last but not least, for
the developer experience,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.780
it deploys instantaneously.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.470
You have a YAML-based
declarative language.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:33.100
You can also use JSON,
actually, with variables,

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.550
with expressions and formulas.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:40.150
You can apply some conditional
logic for conditional step

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:43.750
execution and branching
into your workflow steps

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:45.250
with, what else?

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:46.630
Built-in JSON parsing.

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:50.680
When you need to parse the
API output from the API

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:54.100
that you call with
error and retry

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:57.850
logic when an API fails and
you need to retry and call it

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:01.880
again, and various other
useful built-in functions.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:04.990
There's also built in
authentication for Google Cloud

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.310
APIs and services.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.700
So for example,
services that would

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.370
be hosted on Cloud Run
or Cloud Functions,

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.400
are pretty much
transparently authenticated

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.730
when called from the
workflow definition.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.670
It executes workflows
with the reliability

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:25.240
required for enterprise and
line of business applications.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.420
Workflows offers fast scheduling
of workflow executions

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:31.810
and transition between the
various tasks and steps.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.780
And also, predictable
performance with no cold

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:35.800
start at all.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:37.390
In terms of pricing,
as I said, it's

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.940
a per step execution
pricing approach.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.230
So let's have a look
at the demonstration.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.970
It's an expense
report application.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:50.420
We'll use Document AI for the
smart parsing of the receipts,

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:54.710
Cloud Functions for some logic,
for invoking the workflow,

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:58.460
calling callback endpoints
for the manager approval

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:02.060
or also transforming some
light ETL for transforming

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.750
some complex data into
simpler data structure that

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.980
will store in Cloud
Firestore as structured data.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.830
And, of course, Workflows to
orchestrate the whole process.

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.380
So let's have a look
at the demonstration.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.650
Here is our expense
report application.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:23.010
As an employee, I have
access to this page where

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:27.330
I can select and upload some
receipts to create my expense

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:28.000
report.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.350
So let's click on
the Select button.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:35.790
I'm going to pick
a couple receipts.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.620
Then I'm going to click
the Submit Report button.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.650
So the receipts
are being uploaded,

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:45.780
and the report is submitted.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.710
And now it's already in
the processing queue.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:53.550
It's going to be handled
by Document AI who's

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:58.950
going to look at the items, the
line items, the supplier, et

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:02.830
cetera, that are recognized
in those receipts.

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:06.810
Now, let's have a look at the
administration page, which

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:09.660
is the page that
managers will be

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:14.610
able to look at to
accept, reject, approve

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:15.820
expense reports.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:20.130
So here we already have one
report waiting in the queue.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.010
So the previous report
that was submitted

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.630
contained two receipts.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.280
I can click to see the details.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.370
So there's the Raintree
Restaurant and the Raphael

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:33.360
Restaurant, and I can see some
details of what was ordered,

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:37.080
the price, the total
amount, the currency.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.870
And Document AI is able
to do some data enrichment

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:44.160
so I could potentially
see the knowledge graph

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:47.610
information, for
example, with the details

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.320
about the address
of the restaurant.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:53.910
Then, if I'm happy with
this expense report,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.450
I can just click on
Approve or, if I'm unhappy,

00:15:57.450 --> 00:15:59.520
I can click on Reject.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:01.650
And all those
actions are actually

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:05.820
going through the Workflow,
the Workflow definition.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.580
And for example, now I
can see that my report

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:11.130
came into the approved section.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.470
That's the one that
I just approved.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:15.840
And, yeah.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:20.190
The one I had submitted just
a few seconds ago arrived.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.790
This time, the vendor wasn't
recognized, but at least

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:30.460
the amount, the currency and
the details were handled nicely.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:34.313
So let's have a look at how
it's done under the hood.

00:16:34.313 --> 00:16:35.730
So first of all,
I'm going to show

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.650
you the Workflow definition.

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:41.980
So as I said, everything
is going to the Workflow,

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:43.300
or through the Workflow.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.750
So whenever there's
an action in the UI,

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:51.250
it's going through some Cloud
Function invoking the Workflow.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.720
So, for example, here,
I'm preparing my workflow

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.750
definition with some
variables, but let's have

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.000
a look at this one.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.260
With Workflow you can
call REST APIs, any API,

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:08.040
whether it's from
Google, your own APIs,

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:11.130
or it could be third
party external APIs.

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:14.970
But here, I'm not calling
Document AI directly,

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:17.400
I'm actually
calling a connector,

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.730
which is a dedicated
wrapper, run

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:23.520
the REST API for Document AI.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.230
And what's great with that
is that, although invoking

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:31.320
the batch process of going
through all the receipts,

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.920
I don't have to handle
the waiting, logic,

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.960
queuing, et cetera.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:42.210
I just need to say, OK, I'm
submitting all those receipts.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.340
They are here.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.610
You'll find everything
there, and I'm

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.470
interested in the results.

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:55.350
Please put the results of
the processed annotations

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:57.810
in Cloud Storage.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:01.200
So in a few lines, we're
able to call Document AI.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:04.980
And then, so either it's
working or whether there's

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.090
an error potentially.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.690
So we might branch in different
places of our workflow.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:13.680
And by the way, you can see the
visualization of your workflow

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:16.500
on the right side here.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:21.240
I'm updating Firestore to
explain what the status is.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:24.900
But here, if the
parsing succeeded,

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:30.510
we can have a look at the
annotations, the results

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:32.100
returned by Document AI.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.500
And we have a little
Cloud Function here

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:38.880
that's going to parse the JSON
file generated by Document AI

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:43.710
to find the relevant
details I'm interested in.

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:47.400
So this function,
process annotations,

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:51.060
and what it's doing
here is that I'm

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.970
going to tell this Cloud
Function, OK, let's

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:57.030
have a look at the files
in this storage bucket

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:03.060
where the JSON generated
by Document AI is located.

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:07.140
Then I'm going to go
through all the files.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.030
Each receipt has a file
associated with it.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:12.060
So I'm downloading
the file locally,

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.620
and I'm passing it with JSON.

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:17.670
And then I'm going to hand
for interesting fields.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:21.930
For example, I'm curious
about the supplier name.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:25.350
I want to learn about all the
line items that are available,

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:26.430
each line.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:30.060
I ordered a pizza,
I order some drink.

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:33.402
I'm also interested in
the total amount of money,

00:19:33.402 --> 00:19:34.860
but also the
currency, because it's

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.260
important to know which
currency was used.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.000
And then I'm
creating this summary

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.180
and then I'm storing
that in Firestore.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:45.990
All the metadata, I'm
storing that in Firestore.

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:53.470
And let's go to Firestore where
I can see the results here.

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:58.450
So what's stored in Firestore,
that's things like the status,

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:01.530
whether here it's
awaiting validation,

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:05.550
but here I see the line
items for this first receipt,

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:08.640
the line items for
the older receipts.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.250
But if you come
back here, you'll

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:13.800
notice that the report
is awaiting validation

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:18.060
because we want the manager to
approve or reject this report.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.510
So that's why we have the
Approve and Reject button.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:23.110
But what's happening here?

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:25.260
We've got the workflow
that is running,

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:28.140
but there's a very useful
feature in Workflow

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.360
that's called callbacks.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:34.980
A callback is a way to wait
for some external input,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.320
whether it's a human input.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:41.440
Here, it's the manager that's
going to approve this report.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:43.470
But it could be
some external system

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:46.230
that is going to call
back the workflow

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.690
to continue its execution.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:52.410
And let me show you this
part of the workflow.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:55.060
I'm creating a callback here.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:21:02.940
So I'm saying, I'm ready to
receive an HTTP post on the URL

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:06.040
that workflow is going
to generate for me.

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:10.500
And then I'm also going to
wait for the workflow to be--

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:12.420
the callback to be called.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.330
So this is here, await_callback.

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:20.130
And I'm waiting just an
hour, but I could wait longer

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:22.440
potentially, up to one year.

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.680
And then either it works
or not, it's rejected,

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:27.840
or perhaps there's an error
and I update Firestore

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.460
with this information.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:34.530
And that's where here, again,
I interact with Firestore

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:39.550
to store the approval
or rejection.

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:42.910
So that's about it for the demo.

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.840
So to recap what you
saw in this demo, here's

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:48.630
an architecture diagram
that sums it up.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:54.420
So we have the two web pages
for the employee submitting

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:58.240
the expense report and for the
manager approving the report.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:03.270
So the first step is to upload
the receipts into Cloud Storage

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:03.900
buckets.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:07.290
We'll do that thanks
to Firebase Storage,

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:10.560
which uploads the document
into Cloud Storage buckets.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:14.827
Then, again, from the UI
from the employee view,

00:22:14.827 --> 00:22:16.410
we'll go through a
Cloud Function that

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.890
is going to invoke workflows
to create an execution

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:23.496
and stop the execution
of our workflow.

00:22:23.496 --> 00:22:24.970
What Workflow is going to do--

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:31.470
well, it's going to store
some data about the expense

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.260
report that's been
submitted, but then it's

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:39.090
going to invoke Document AI
and let Document AI fetch

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:42.750
the receipts from Cloud
Storage, analyze the receipts

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:45.570
and then store the
result into a JSON file

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:49.800
in another bucket, a
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:54.150
Then, the workflow is going
to call another function whose

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:57.060
job is going to actually
go through the JSON data

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.370
and pick up just the
right information we're

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.990
interested in.

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:03.574
Line items, supplier name,
amount, total amounts,

00:23:03.574 --> 00:23:05.790
currency, et cetera.

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:07.290
So we've got the data.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:10.020
In the manager view,
the manager will

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:14.790
be able to click on either
buttons, the one approving

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:17.550
the expense report or the one
rejecting the expense report.

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:20.430
And we'll go through the
callback, the callback URL.

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:23.700
From this web page, we'll
call back into the workflow

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.220
through a Cloud
Function to continue

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:29.070
the execution of the
workflow and store

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:32.820
the result, approved or
rejected, in our Cloud

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:34.590
Firestore database.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:37.650
And that's about it for
this architecture diagram

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.140
of our expense
report application.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.080
So thanks a lot
for your attention.

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:50.080
We'd like to encourage you to
have a look at those few links

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:54.280
here to get started on Document
AI, Workflows and Cloud

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:55.030
Functions.

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:58.240
We are really looking
forward to hearing from you

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:02.015
about what you're going to build
next with those technologies.

